WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.520 --> 00:00:07.200
A list of the most influential people of all time&nbsp;
wouldn't be complete without Sir Isaac Newton.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:12.320
Newton's laws are tied to almost everything we&nbsp;
see in everyday life. They help us understand how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:19.120
cars work, how baseballs move, how anything moves.&nbsp;
Yet there was a secret side to the 17th century&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:24.720
scientist which will become clear by the end&nbsp;
of this story. By his mid-20s, Newton had already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:30.160
invented calculus. Not bad for the son of an&nbsp;
illiterate farmer. He would never meet his father&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:35.760
who died three months before his birth on&nbsp;
Christmas day 1642 in the small english village&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:41.600
of Woolsthorpe Manor in the county of Lincolnshire.&nbsp;
He was a premature baby and so small he was later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:47.840
told he could fit inside a quart mug. He barely&nbsp;
survived. When Newton was three, his mom remarried&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:52.720
and moved to a nearby village to live with a&nbsp;
wealthy clergyman. Newton was left behind in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:58.960
care of his grandparents. That abandonment scarred&nbsp;
him. Years later, he wrote a list of his sins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:03.920
recalling an outburst from childhood: "Threatening&nbsp;
my father and mother Smith to burn them and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:09.680
house over them." He was a curious child - more&nbsp;
interested in mechanics than making friends.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:15.200
He carved this sundial as a nine-year-old. When&nbsp;
he was 12, he enrolled at a local grammar school/ &nbsp;

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:19.280
His signature can still be seen by a&nbsp;
windowsill of the King's School today.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:23.280
After the death of his stepdad, his mother&nbsp;
tried to remove him from school so he could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:28.480
be a farmer - a prospect that he dreaded. Luckily,&nbsp;
the schoolmaster convinced his mother to send&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:33.200
him back to class where he gained the knowledge&nbsp;
necessary to enter the University of Cambridge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:39.040
in 1661, paying his way by working as a valet.&nbsp;
Although he studied the works of the ancient&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:43.600
Greek philosophers, he questioned their&nbsp;
theories writing in his notebook in Latin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:49.760
"Plato is my friend, Aristotle is my friend, but&nbsp;
my best friend is truth." He vowed to find the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:56.000
truth through rigorous scientific experimentation.&nbsp;
His time as an undergrad was unremarkable but the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:01.120
extraordinary circumstances that happened next&nbsp;
would set the stage for his greatest achievements.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:08.800
Shortly after receiving his Bachelor's degree&nbsp;
in 1665, the bubonic plague ravaged Europe and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:13.120
would take the lives of an estimated&nbsp;
one out of every four people in London.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:17.200
The pandemic forced Newton to work from&nbsp;
his childhood home for the next two years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:22.640
and would lead to his most amazing breakthroughs.&nbsp;
He used a prism to discover that white light&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:25.840
and sunlight is made up of&nbsp;
the colors of the rainbow.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:30.880
The widely held belief at the time was Aristotle's&nbsp;
theory that color was a mix of black and white.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:36.080
To prove his theory, Newton built a reflecting&nbsp;
telescope that used mirrors rather than lenses -&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:40.880
leading to a more accurate image. That's a whole&nbsp;
lot safer than the time he stuck a sewing needle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:46.160
into his eye socket to figure out if altering his&nbsp;
eye shape would change his perception of color.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:53.600
Outside the family home was also an apple tree. The&nbsp;
famous apple tree. Legend has it that Newton was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:58.320
sitting beneath a tree when an apple bonked him on&nbsp;
the head, prompting him to think about gravity - the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:03.600
force that brings things down. There's no evidence&nbsp;
to suggest the fruit actually fell on his head&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:09.040
but he did ask the question that helped unlock our&nbsp;
understanding of the universe: Could the same force&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:14.560
reach all the way to the moon? He reasoned that&nbsp;
the same gravitational pull kept the moon orbiting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:19.120
around Earth rather than wandering off and he&nbsp;
believed this could also explain the movement of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:24.400
our planets in the solar system. The mathematics at&nbsp;
the time wasn't sophisticated enough to determine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:30.560
the motion of these objects so Newton invented&nbsp;
his own form of math calculus. There was a dispute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:35.760
over who actually invented calculus. When German&nbsp;
mathematician Gottfried Leibniz published his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:42.240
paper on calculus in 1684, Newton claimed he'd&nbsp;
done the same work 20 years earlier. The thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:46.720
is, Newton was so secretive that he hadn't actually&nbsp;
made his efforts public because he couldn't stand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:52.320
the scrutiny of his work. When Leibniz appealed&nbsp;
to the Royal Society in London, Newton wielded his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:57.840
influence as the scientific academy's president to&nbsp;
get it to side with him. Most historians agree that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:04.240
the two discovered calculus independently. In 1667,&nbsp;
after the end of the plague, he returned to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:10.800
University to continue his research as a fellow.&nbsp;
He was a workaholic. Sometimes, he'd forget to eat. &nbsp;

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:16.320
Just two years later while still in his mid-twenties,&nbsp;
he obtained one of the most prestigious academic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:21.200
posts in the world. The Lucasian Professor&nbsp;
of Mathematics later held by none other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:27.040
then Stephen Hawking. Yet he was indifferent to his&nbsp;
students. One time, when no one showed up for class,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:34.800
he is said to have lectured to an empty room. His&nbsp;
true passion lay in research. In 1687, he published&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:40.480
his masterpiece: the Mathematical Principles of&nbsp;
Natural Philosophy - often referred to simply as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:46.560
Principia - one of the most important works ever&nbsp;
written. This was his own first edition copy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:51.200
It was here that he laid out his law of universal&nbsp;
gravitation as well as his three laws of motion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:57.360
The first describes how an object acts when no&nbsp;
force is acting upon it. For example, a rocket stays&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.360 --> 00:05:02.640
still until a force is applied to it. The second&nbsp;
law tells us that the more mass an object has,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:07.680
the more force it'll take to move it. A larger&nbsp;
rocket requires more fuel to make it accelerate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:13.440
The final law states that for every action there&nbsp;
is a reaction. The engine produces hot exhaust&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:18.960
gases that flow through the rear of the rocket. In&nbsp;
reaction, a thrusting force is produced - pushing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:23.840
rocket skywards. Newton may be considered one of&nbsp;
the greatest scientists the world has ever known&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:30.480
yet he actually contributed more words to theology.&nbsp;
Newton believed the Bible provided the code to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:35.760
natural world and by carefully studying the Holy&nbsp;
texts, he could predict the future. He believed the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:41.760
apocalypse would happen in the year 2060 with the&nbsp;
caveat: "It may end later but I see no reason for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:47.360
its ending sooner." He foresaw a period marked by&nbsp;
war and catastrophe followed by the second coming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:52.960
of Christ and the beginning of a new, divine era. His&nbsp;
religious side was largely hidden from the public&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:59.040
all the way until 1936, when Sotheby's auctioned&nbsp;
off his theological manuscripts. They ended up in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:05.120
the hands of a Jewish scholar before being given&nbsp;
to the state of Israel. 7,500 pages in Newton's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:10.960
own handwriting. He did hold unorthodox views of&nbsp;
Christianity by rejecting the Trinity - the belief&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:16.000
in the father, son, and the holy spirit. And also&nbsp;
fought against the attempt by King James II to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:21.360
catholicize the universities which got him elected&nbsp;
as a Member of Parliament where he served two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:26.800
brief terms. Apparently, the only thing he said on&nbsp;
the record was to request that a window be closed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:33.840
Newton also had another unknown side to him. He&nbsp;
spent 25 years secretly studying alchemy - the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:38.640
search for a method to turn common metals into&nbsp;
gold. This is a manuscript where Newton wrote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:43.600
down a recipe thought to be a step toward&nbsp;
concocting the mythical Philosopher's Stone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.600 --> 00:06:48.480
now popularized in the Harry Potter series.
Alchemists believed it could even help humans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:54.000
achieve immortality. Unfortunately, Newton may have&nbsp;
gotten mercury poisoning from all the time spent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:59.680
in the laboratory. Examinations of his hair after&nbsp;
his death found high levels of the toxic compound&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.680 --> 00:07:05.840
which scholars believe could explain his mental&nbsp;
breakdown in 1693 when he lost grip on reality.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.840 --> 00:07:10.480
He wrote letters accusing the few friends he&nbsp;
had of conspiring against him. He suffered from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:16.240
insomnia and oppression. The personal crisis&nbsp;
lasted a year and a half. Not too long after he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:22.800
ended his 30-year career at Cambridge. In 1696,&nbsp;
he moved to lLndon to help run the Royal Mint.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:27.680
Britain's finances were in ruins because of the&nbsp;
rampant practice of clipping off pieces of coins.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.240 --> 00:07:33.360
Counterfeiting was also an issue. Newton used a&nbsp;
scientific precision to improve the accuracy of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:39.520
coinmaking as Warden and then Master of the Mint.&nbsp;
He also took it upon himself to prosecute culprits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:44.400
some of whom ended up hanging from the gallows. His&nbsp;
later years would be spent further cementing his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:49.120
reputation and sometimes that meant trying&nbsp;
to erase his rivals from the history books.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:54.320
Another ugly dispute involved a brilliant&nbsp;
scientist named Robert Hooke who contended&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.320 --> 00:07:58.800
he was the one who gave Newton the notion that&nbsp;
led to his theory of gravity and wanted credit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.800 --> 00:08:03.200
In response, Newton is accused of using his&nbsp;
powers as President of the Royal Society&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.200 --> 00:08:09.360
to get rid of the only known portrait of Hooke.&nbsp;
None exists to this day. Newton succeeded in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:15.120
getting the legacy he wanted. In 1705, Queen Anne&nbsp;
of England knighted him during a royal visit to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:21.120
the University of Cambridge, making him Sir Isaac&nbsp;
Newton. A complicated man who remained reclusive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:26.560
yet desperately wanted to be remembered. Who threw&nbsp;
himself into his work at the cost of all hobbies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.560 --> 00:08:33.120
and never married. Who was a man of science and&nbsp;
also, a man of faith. Newton died in his sleep on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:40.000
March 20, 1727 and was buried at Westminster Abbey.&nbsp;
the Latin inscription on his grave reads: Here

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:46.400
lies that which was mortal of Isaac Newton. His&nbsp;
immortal legacy continues to mold our modern world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.400 --> 00:08:51.360
The English poet Alexander Pope was so moved&nbsp;
by Newton's accomplishments, he wrote the famous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:58.480
epitaph: Nature and nature's laws lay hid&nbsp;in
night. God said "Let Newton be" and all was light.

00:09:01.280 --> 00:09:04.400
For Newsthink, I'm Cindy Pom.

